.bp
.in 0
.ig
.ul 1
..
2.  HARDWARE

.ti 3
For those who find hardware all very confusing or for those who find it
crystal clear this section should best be skipped as it becomes slightly
technical but not oppressively so and as such would not satisfy either of
the above mentioned groups.


.ig
.ul 1
..
2.1 CYBER

.ti 3
There are two mainframe computers installed at UNSW - a CDC Cyber 72-26
and a CDC Cyber 171-18.
The '72 was the original machine and took all the processing load.
Since it had to support both timesharing and batch processing (both
being somewhat incompatible with each other), response time and turnaround
were bad and so the '171 was added later
on.

.ti 3
We need only consider the Cyber 171 since it is the Batch machine.

.in 7
.ig
.ul 1
..
CPU's (central processing units)

.ti 10
The '171 has one CPU, and this runs the batch jobs, and also part
of the executive for EXPORT.

.ig
.ul 1
..
PPU's (peripheral processing units)

.ti 10
The '171 has ten of these.
One of the PPU's runs the major proportion of the batch station
service software which drives
the 6671 multiplexer to which your batch station is connected.
In theory the PPU (peripheral processing unit) can cope with sixteen remote
batch stations.

.ig
.ul 1
..
6671 (16 port 4800 baud terminal multiplexer)

.ti 10
This is what interfaces the PPU to the communications line from the remote
terminal.
.ti 10
The 6671 is polled from The PPU (it does not interrupt the PPU) as often as the
program running in the PPU can manage.
At 4800 baud the 6671 requires service at least every
1.5 ms or data may be lost. It may not always get the service in time
and this would lead to transmission errors in data from the batch station to the CYBER.

.in 0
.ig
.ul 1
..
2.2 MODEMS (G.E.C. 4800 baud)

.ti 3
This is the little brown box in the larger green box (that
lives in the house that ........sorry) on the wall in your batch station.
.ti 3
Without going into electrical details, a modem
is a device such that if you put one at both ends of a communication line
as below
signals entering at one end of the line appear at the opposite end unchanged.
.ss
.ne 8


		-------				-------
 in/out	--------(modem)-----------line----------(modem)-------- in/out
		-------				-------



.ds
.ti 3
Since all data up and down the line must pass through these modems
a fault in one or both of them will lead to data transmission errors.

.ig
.ul 1
..
2.3 PDP 11/40

.ti 3
The PDP 11/40 installed in your batch station is one of about a dozen
all over the university. As a batch station operator this is the computer
you must live with every day. In order to operate the computer properly
under all circumstances you should understand something about the
hardware that surrounds you.
.ti 3
The following subsections should give you this understanding.

.in 7
.ig
.ul 1
..
2.3.1 CPU

.ti 10
The 11/40 is a 16 bit
parallel machine having a modular design arranged
around a central communications 'bus' called UNIBUS.
UNIBUS provides an information transfer path between any two modules.
.ti 10
Since all system elements, including the CPU, communicate with each other
in identical fashion via UNIBUS, the processor has the
same access to peripheral devices as it has to memory. Memory is just another
device.
.ti 10
The instruction set reflects the modular bus-connected
design of the 11/40 having no distinction between memory reference
instructions or i/o instructions.
.ti 10
The 11/40 has a multi-level priority interrupt system which
permits the processor to respond automatically to asynchronous events
occurring at any device.
Any number of separate devices can be attached to any level.
The levels range from the lowest priority of zero to the highest of seven.
.ti 10
The processor continually (after each instruction) compares its own
programmable priority with that of any interrupting devices and acknowledges
the device with the highest level above the processors priority level.
Each device in the 11/40 system has a hardware pointer to
its own interrupt vector (two memory words) . One word points to the
starting address of the service routine for that device
and the other contains the new processor status information (including
priority) with which the service routine is to executed.
When an interrupt is acknowledged the processor automatically begins execution
of the service routine specified by the interrupt vector.
.ti 10
Servicing an interrupt for a device can be interrupted for servicing a higher
priority device. Servicing of the lower priority device will be resumed
automatically upon completion of the higher level servicing.

.ig
.ul 1
..
2.3.2 Memory

.ti 10
All the 11/40s on campus have memory, although as with many things, some
have more than oThers. the smaller batch stations have 8K (16 bit
word, K=1024) and the souped up batch stations have up to 104k.
.ti 10
Actually the memories in the 11/40s are very like the CYBER memory because
they dont have parity checking either (the option costs an extra $3000 
per 64k words).

.ig
.ul 1
..
2.3.3 Console

.ti 10
Each batch station has at least one video display terminal connected
to their 11/40, more than likely a VT05 type.
This is used for communication between the batch station operator
and programs running on his 11/40.

.ig
.ul 1
..
2.3.4 Card Reader

.ti 10
All batch stations are equipped with at least one card reader,
usually one manufactured by CDC (the blue, cream and brushed aluminium ones).
.ti 10
The CDC type use an optical reading system to detect
black holes (astronomers note!) on the back of the cards but cannot
differentiate between writing and punching.
They are designed to run at one of four speeds (300, 400, 600 and 800
cards per minute) set by internal adjustments.
Most are set at 600 cards per minute.
.ti 10
Unfortunately most batch station operators whose stations do not have discs
have never seen their card readers
operating at anything like 600 cpm.
.ti 10
The standard card readers interrupt every column of the card (750nS)
which is a rather heavy load on the C.P.U.
.ti 10
A new interface providing buffering of a complete card has been
designed by V. Kuperman (CSU) and is in operation
at most batch stations.
This reduces interrupts to every 75mS.

.ig
.ul 1
..
2.3.5 Printer

.ti 10
All batch stations are equipped with at least one line printer,
usually one manufactured by CDC (those noisy great monsters
in grey and cream).
.ti 10
The CDC type are a 400 line per minute (there are two 600 lpm
printers on campus) drum printer.
They print 136 columns across the page, but to save
money they only have half this number of hammers.
This accounts for the comic shuffling of the paper
while it is printing.
.ti 10
Unfortunately, as with the card reader, the printer is seldom
seen to work at the rated lines per minute
at a station without discs (under UNIX
they have been known to operate faster than their rating).

.ig
.ul 1
..
2.3.6 DP-11 (DEC Synchronous Communications Port)

.ti 10
This device is the interface between the 11/40 processor and the
CYBER communications line.
It is a level five priority interrupt device providing full duplex
(communications up and down the line simultaneously; it never gets to use it) data transmission
at a range of speeds up to 9600 bits per second.
.ti 10
While receiving and transmitting data, the DP-11 also monitors
the line for certain types of data errors.

.ig
.ul 1
..
2.3.7 Discs

.ti 10
These determine whether your batch station runs like a bat out of hell
or acts as if it has been caught in the eternal fires.
.ti 10
The only type installed in batch stations on campus are the RK05 type.
These provide online storage of a paltry 2.5 Mbytes (characters)
per disc at a rather slow speed. The availability of this storage is the factor which leads to the
increased speed in reading cards and printing printout under UNIX.
Faster and larger discs would improve this even further.
.ti 10
Use of discs for spooling (discussed later) enables the CYBER
communications line to be used more efficiently.
Under UNIX the discs may also be used
to support local batch processing and a multi-user terminal system on the PDP 11/40.

.ig
.ul 1
..
2.3.8 Air Conditioners

.ti 10
This section has been included at the request of a certain
temperature sensitive
Senior Lecturer in Computer Science.
.ti 10
All batch stations should be airconditioned !
.ti 10
A few actually are !
.ti 10
Some of them adequately even !
.ti 10
Although Digital Equipment has a wide environmental operating
specification your computer will run better and longer between failures
if it is kept at a stable moderate temperature and low humidity.
Air conditioners also help keep disc killing dust to a minimum.
.ti 10
The air conditioning installed at the various batch stations falls into three
main categories.
.ti 10
The first is provided by the main building plant which has plenty
of reserve capacity and gives good service most of the time.
Batch stations in the newer
buildings have this benefit due to their location. Not so the others.
.ti 10
The second is provided by expensive semi-industrial units
having split cooling and heat dissipation units.
These are designed to serve one or a few rooms and do so well,
when they are operating.
Unfortunately experiences with these units have not been
inspiring.
.ti 10
The final, cheapest (but not necessarily the best) and most common units
are self contained 2HP window or wall mounted used in most other batch stations.
These are designed to serve one small room only, and do so moderately well,
however as they must be running at full capacity most of the time they
tend to fail occasionally.
Usually the fan motor bearings seize.
Although the manufacturers say this can be avoided by routine maintenance
(oiling the bearings every few months) they neglect to mention
that the unit must be almost dismantled to get at the oil holes!
.ti 10
The university air conditioning section has been advised to
give priority to batch stations and computer room units,
but what can be done with a blown unit but send it back to
its maker with pleadings for a speedy return.
.ti 10
This then is the situation you must live with.

.nx 6om3.0
